PREAMBLE
The advancement of Artificial Intelligence represents one of the defining technological transformations of the twenty-first century. Its continued development demands not only scientific excellence but also principled leadership founded upon the enduring Stoic virtues of wisdom, justice, courage, and temperance.
Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech affirms that technological innovation must remain inseparable from ethical governance, legal accountability, digital sovereignty, cybersecurity resilience, and the preservation of human dignity.
This framework establishes the intellectual property rights, licensing principles, collaborative governance standards, and policy expectations governing all proprietary works, software, research, documentation, methodologies, algorithms, system architectures, technical publications, and derivative innovations developed by or on behalf of Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech.
The objective of this framework is to strengthen lawful innovation while encouraging responsible international collaboration between governments, academia, industry, independent researchers, civil society, and technology providers operating within the principles of democratic governance, international law, and corporate responsibility.
 
ARTICLE I
COPYRIGHT OWNERSHIP
All intellectual property created, designed, authored, engineered, documented, researched, or otherwise developed by Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech shall remain the exclusive property of Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech unless expressly transferred through a written agreement executed by authorised representatives.
This protection includes, but is not limited to:
•	Software source code
•	Artificial intelligence models
•	Machine learning architectures
•	Algorithms
•	Security frameworks
•	Technical specifications
•	Research methodologies
•	Publications
•	System designs
•	Databases
•	Digital assets
•	Documentation
•	Business methodologies
•	Strategic policy frameworks
•	Trade secrets
•	Proprietary engineering concepts
No ownership rights shall be inferred through hosting, deployment, testing, integration, or collaborative evaluation unless expressly assigned in writing.
 
ARTICLE II
COPYRIGHT RECOGNITION
Any organisation hosting, evaluating, integrating, testing, or commercially utilising technologies originating from Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech is requested, and where governed by contractual agreement shall be required, to recognise the original developer and acknowledge the proprietary ownership of the intellectual property.
Copyright correspondence relating to ownership verification shall be directed to:
Copyright Office
ziamziamtech@gmail.com
Administrative correspondence:
alphaquadron@gmail.com
 
ARTICLE III
LICENSING PRINCIPLES
Unless expressly authorised through a separate written licence agreement, all software, documentation, research materials, technical frameworks, and associated intellectual property are licensed solely for authorised evaluation or agreed commercial purposes.
No licence grants ownership.
No licence permits redistribution, resale, sublicensing, reverse engineering, replication, or derivative commercial development without prior written approval from Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech.
Any authorised licence shall remain subject to applicable copyright legislation, contractual obligations, international intellectual property law, and relevant digital sovereignty regulations.
 
ARTICLE IV
CONTRACTUAL ENGAGEMENT
Where an organisation intends to deploy, integrate, commercialise, or significantly develop technologies originating from Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech, the parties should negotiate an appropriate written agreement before production deployment.
Such agreements should establish:
•	Intellectual property ownership
•	Licensing scope
•	Revenue arrangements where applicable
•	Confidentiality obligations
•	Information security responsibilities
•	Liability allocation
•	Compliance requirements
•	Dispute resolution procedures
•	Termination rights
•	Ongoing governance obligations
This approach promotes transparency, responsible innovation, commercial certainty, and sound corporate governance.
 
ARTICLE V
SHARED RESPONSIBILITY IN ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE
The development of Artificial Intelligence within a free and democratic society requires a doctrine of shared responsibility.
Every participating organisation should contribute toward:
•	Information security
•	Privacy protection
•	Cyber resilience
•	Ethical engineering
•	Transparent governance
•	Human oversight
•	Responsible innovation
•	Regulatory compliance
•	Respect for digital sovereignty
Security must not be treated as a post-development activity but as a foundational engineering discipline integrated throughout the complete system development lifecycle.
 
ARTICLE VI
GLOBAL RESEARCH COLLABORATION
Innovation flourishes through cooperation rather than isolation.
Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech supports constructive collaboration among:
•	Universities
•	Research institutions
•	Independent researchers
•	Government agencies
•	Technology companies
•	International organisations
•	Civil society
Independent innovation should be recognised on its own merits, and where proprietary contributions are identified, reasonable efforts should be undertaken to establish communication with the originating developer in accordance with applicable intellectual property laws and contractual obligations.
 
ARTICLE VII
SAFETY, ETHICS, AND SYSTEM RESILIENCE
Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech advocates the integration of mandatory safety and ethics assessments throughout the engineering lifecycle.
Accordingly, participating organisations are encouraged to implement:
•	Continuous security reviews
•	Ethical impact assessments
•	Vulnerability management
•	Independent technical audits
•	Risk-based governance
•	Incident reporting mechanisms
•	Organisational learning frameworks
Proactive resilience is a superior governance strategy to reactive crisis management.
 
ARTICLE VIII
RISK-BASED ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE GOVERNANCE
Artificial Intelligence regulation should reflect the principle of proportionality.
Restrictions should be calibrated according to demonstrable levels of risk while preserving legitimate scientific inquiry, education, healthcare innovation, environmental research, engineering advancement, and other socially beneficial applications.
Public education concerning the capabilities, limitations, and responsible use of Artificial Intelligence should remain an essential component of national digital resilience.
 
ARTICLE IX
DIGITAL SOVEREIGNTY
Digital sovereignty requires that Artificial Intelligence systems operate consistently with applicable national legislation, international obligations, cybersecurity requirements, privacy protections, and human rights principles.
Cross-border deployment of Artificial Intelligence technologies should respect:
•	National sovereignty
•	Data governance laws
•	Privacy legislation
•	Cybersecurity regulations
•	International cooperation frameworks
•	Corporate accountability
•	Regulatory transparency
Responsible innovation requires balancing technological progress with lawful oversight.
 
ARTICLE X
TECHNOLOGY, SECURITY, AND INTERNATIONAL COOPERATION
Artificial Intelligence should be developed to strengthen public safety while respecting international humanitarian law, human rights, and democratic governance.
Technology alone cannot resolve ideological conflict, violent extremism, or terrorism.
Long-term solutions require coordinated efforts involving governments, researchers, educators, international organisations, diplomatic institutions, community leaders, and technology providers.
Accordingly, Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech supports continued cooperation with governmental institutions, international organisations, humanitarian agencies, academic institutions, and industry partners to improve cybersecurity, prevent online radicalisation, strengthen digital resilience, and encourage evidence-based approaches to emerging technological risks.
 
ARTICLE XI
INDUSTRY COOPERATION
Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech encourages the establishment of voluntary international cooperation mechanisms through which technology organisations may exchange anonymised technical information relating to:
•	System failures
•	Security vulnerabilities
•	Recovery methodologies
•	Defensive engineering practices
•	Safety research
•	Cyber resilience
Such collaboration should always respect confidentiality obligations, intellectual property rights, competition law, and applicable legal requirements.
 
ARTICLE XII
LIMITATION OF LICENCE
Except where otherwise agreed in writing, this licence does not grant:
•	Ownership rights
•	Patent rights
•	Trademark rights
•	Commercial exploitation rights
•	Rights to create derivative proprietary products
•	Rights to redistribute protected materials
All rights not expressly granted remain reserved by Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech.
 
GOVERNING PRINCIPLES
This framework is founded upon:
•	Rule of Law
•	Corporate Governance
•	Academic Integrity
•	Ethical Engineering
•	Information Security
•	Digital Sovereignty
•	Scientific Excellence
•	Human Rights
•	Responsible Innovation
•	International Cooperation
•	Transparency
•	Accountability
•	Shared Responsibility
 
CLOSING DECLARATION
Ziam-Ziam Cyber Tech respectfully invites governments, universities, technology enterprises, research institutions, independent innovators, and international partners to participate in constructive dialogue concerning the responsible evolution of Artificial Intelligence.
Our vision is founded upon the belief that enduring technological progress is achieved through lawful governance, principled leadership, scientific excellence, ethical responsibility, and international cooperation.
By combining innovation with accountability, humanity may establish an Artificial Intelligence ecosystem that advances knowledge, strengthens cybersecurity, promotes peace, and serves future generations with integrity.
